    "created_at": "2016-08-22T12:35:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-22T12:35:26Z",
    "body": "I think the two are not exactly the same. The \"machine-readable equivalent of the element's contents\" is for UAs to get a value, but the first part you quoted tries to place a document conformance requirement, i.e. the conforming syntax. So it's actually covered by\n\n> The datetime value of a time element must match one of the following syntaxes.\n\nbut possibly it's also trying to take it a step further and require that the \"non-machine-readable\" content, so to say, matches the value of the `datetime` attribute. For instance, the following would be non-conforming:\n\n```\n<time datetime=\"2016-08-22\">22nd August of 1999</time>\n```\n\n(Automated conformance checkers can't check such requirements, but there are various similar requirements in HTML, e.g. requirements on `img alt`...)\n\nPossibly there are tweaks we can do to make this less confusing. Any ideas?\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
